WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.571
String-library choices vary by platform,
compiler, C library implementation,

2
00:00:04.651 --> 00:00:07.046
coding standard, and deployment
environment.

3
00:00:07.126 --> 00:00:10.120
A function available on one target may not
exist on another.

4
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:13.912
A function with a similar name may have
different return-value behavior,

5
00:00:13.992 --> 00:00:15.968
truncation rules, or parameter order.

6
00:00:16.048 --> 00:00:18.982
Portability needs to be part of the
selection discussion.

7
00:00:19.062 --> 00:00:23.492
Standard functions are widely available,
but many require careful caller discipline.

8
00:00:23.572 --> 00:00:27.225
Optional bounds-checking interfaces may be
present only under certain

9
00:00:27.305 --> 00:00:28.981
implementations or build modes.

10
00:00:29.061 --> 00:00:31.815
BSD-style APIs, platform-specific secure
variants,

11
00:00:31.895 --> 00:00:34.171
and third-party libraries can all be
useful,

12
00:00:34.251 --> 00:00:36.586
but their contracts are not
interchangeable.

13
00:00:36.666 --> 00:00:39.839
Platform-specific variants often encode
local conventions.

14
00:00:39.919 --> 00:00:42.015
Some report errors through status codes.

15
00:00:42.095 --> 00:00:43.591
Some report required length.

16
00:00:43.671 --> 00:00:46.006
Some guarantee termination in a specific
way.

17
00:00:46.086 --> 00:00:48.002
Some require handler configuration.

18
00:00:48.082 --> 00:00:52.154
A team should document the behavior it
relies on instead of assuming every target

19
00:00:52.234 --> 00:00:54.090
will match one development machine.

20
00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:57.104
Third-party libraries introduce dependency
decisions.

21
00:00:57.184 --> 00:01:00.177
Maintenance, license, platform support,
update cadence,

22
00:01:00.257 --> 00:01:03.191
binary footprint, thread-safety,
allocation strategy,

23
00:01:03.271 --> 00:01:05.008
and integration style all matter.

24
00:01:05.088 --> 00:01:08.201
A string library should fit the product's
operational model,

25
00:01:08.281 --> 00:01:10.017
not just solve one local call site.

26
00:01:10.097 --> 00:01:14.408
Project-owned wrappers can improve
consistency when they preserve safe semantics.

27
00:01:14.488 --> 00:01:18.200
A wrapper should not hide truncation,
discard errors, guess at capacity,

28
00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:21.454
or silently fall back to a weaker behavior
on another platform.

29
00:01:21.534 --> 00:01:24.767
Its contract should be documented and
tested like any other API.

30
00:01:24.847 --> 00:01:28.319
Portability assumptions should be visible
in build configuration,

31
00:01:28.399 --> 00:01:30.555
coding standards, and review checklists.

32
00:01:30.635 --> 00:01:34.467
If a course of action depends on a
platform extension or optional interface,

33
00:01:34.547 --> 00:01:37.780
the project should know what happens on
every supported target.
